// Naive schedule: one worker, no decomposition.
//
// The smoke-test schedule. Every algorithm must produce correct
// output under naive.sched.nuc on every backend before any other
// schedule is allowed to claim correctness.
//
// Single `host`, every kernel placed there, no loop transforms, no
// transfers. With everything on one worker no data crosses worker
// boundaries (PRD §6.3.4); the link pass enforces that. The two
// passes (horizontal blur into `tmp`, then vertical blur of `tmp`
// into `out`) run sequentially on the one worker, exercising the
// intermediate-buffer producer→consumer lifetime this example
// targets.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_image on host;
    place save_image on host;
    place hblur_acc  on host;
    place vblur_acc  on host;

    // No loop transforms.
    // No transfer directives — there are no cross-worker edges.
}
